{
  "root_cause": "When a focused element is removed from the DOM, the focus trap's mutation observer attempts to restore focus to the initial focus node. However, if the removed element had tabindex=0 and there are no elements with positive tabindex in the trap, the focus-in handler incorrectly tries to refocus the detached element before falling back to the initial focus node. Since the detached element is no longer connected, focusing it fails silently, leaving focus on the body.",
  "repair_strategy": "Modify the focus-in handler in index.js to check if the most recently focused node is still connected and focusable before attempting to refocus it. If not, immediately fall back to the initial focus node.",
  "edits": [
    {
      "path": "index.js",
      "sha256": "17ff10072d68a0aabb1e15fdc42b3a1dee1774dc2ced6facc4753e9a5ae89f90",
      "before": "      if (nextNode) {\n        tryFocus(nextNode);\n      } else {\n        tryFocus(state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode || getInitialFocusNode());\n      }",
      "after": "      if (nextNode) {\n        tryFocus(nextNode);\n      } else {\n        const mruNode = state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode;\n        if (mruNode && mruNode.isConnected && isFocusable(mruNode, config.tabbableOptions)) {\n          tryFocus(mruNode);\n        } else {\n          tryFocus(getInitialFocusNode());\n        }\n      }"
    }
  ],
  "expected_accessibility_effect": "Focus will remain trapped within the basket dialog after removing an item, ensuring keyboard users can continue interacting with the dialog without escaping to the page background.",
  "functional_risks": [
    "The isConnected property may not be available in very old browsers, but the focus-trap already assumes modern DOM APIs.",
    "Checking isFocusable adds minimal overhead only in the rare case of focus escape.",
    "Preserves all existing behavior for non-detached MRU nodes."
  ],
  "verification_requirements": [
    "After removing the first basket item via Enter key, focus must remain on the close button or another item in the basket.",
    "Tab navigation must cycle correctly within the basket after item removal.",
    "Closing and reopening the basket must restore initial focus to the close button."
  ]
}

reasoning:
We are given a journey that failed at step 3. The failure message: "Expected state did not occur after action"
The step that failed: 
  {"action":{"key":"Enter","kind":"press"},"assertions":[{"kind":"visible","selector":"#basket","value":true},{"kind":"focus","selector":"#close"}]}
But note: the observed data shows that after pressing Enter on the button with id="remove-1", the focus went to the body (not to #close).

Looking at the observed state:
  After the action (pressing Enter on #remove-1), the focus is on the body (selector: body) and the basket is visible but does not have focus within.

The assertion that failed: 
  {"kind":"visible","selector":"#basket:focus-within","value":true} -> observed: Selector matches 0 elements

This means that after removing the item (by pressing Enter on the remove button for the hand trowel), the focus escaped the basket dialog.

The journey steps leading up to the failure:
  Step 0: seek #open (button to open basket)
  Step 1: press Enter on #open -> opens basket, then assertions: #basket visible and focus on #close
  Step 2: press Tab -> moves focus from #close to #remove-1 (the first item's remove button)
  Step 3: press Enter on #remove-1 -> removes the first item (hand trowel) and then we expect:
          - #count to show "2 items"
          - #basket:focus-within to be true (meaning focus is inside the basket)

But after removing the item, the focus is on the body (outside the basket).

Why did the focus escape?
When we remove the list item (li#item-1) that contained the button (#remove-1) that had focus, the button is removed from the DOM.
The focus trap mechanism (in index.js) should have moved the focus to the next focusable element in the trap when the currently focused element is removed.

Looking at the provided index.js (the focus-trap implementation), we see that there is a mutation observer that checks for removal of the focused node:

  const checkDomRemoval = function (mutations) {
    const isFocusedNodeRemoved = mutations.some(function (mutation) {
      const removedNodes = Array.from(mutation.removedNodes);
      return removedNodes.some(function (node) {
        return node === state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode;
      });
    });

    // If the currently focused is removed then browsers will move focus to the
    // <body> element. If this happens, try to move focus back into the trap.
    if (isFocusedNodeRemoved) {
      tryFocus(getInitialFocusNode());
    }
  };

However, note that in the observed state, after the removal, the focus is on the body. This suggests that the mutation observer did not catch the removal or the tryFocus did not work as expected.

But wait: the observed state shows that the basket is still present and has two items (pruning shears and kneeling pad). The count is updated to "2 items".

Let's look at the HTML after the removal (from the observed state):

  <div class=\"panel\" id=\"basket\" role=\"dialog\" aria-modal=\"true\" aria-labelledby=\"basket-title\">\n    <header>\n      <h2 id=\"basket-title\">Basket</h2>\n      <button id=\"close\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Close basket\">×</button>\n    </header>\n    <p id=\"count\">2 items</p>\n    <ul id=\"items\">\n      \n      <li id=\"item-2\">Pruning shears <button id=\"remove-2\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Remove pruning shears\">Remove</button></li>\n      <li id=\"item-3\">Kneeling pad <button id=\"remove-3\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Remove kneeling pad\">Remove</button></li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>

Note: the first item (item-1) is gone. The button that had focus (remove-1) was inside that li, so it was removed.

The mutation observer should have triggered and called tryFocus(getInitialFocusNode()). What is getInitialFocusNode()?

In the focus trap, when the basket is opened, the initial focus is set to the close button (because we trap focus on the basket and the close button is the first tabbable element?).

But note: the basket's initial focus node is determined by the trap. In the activate function, we call tryFocus(getInitialFocusNode()).

How is getInitialFocusNode() implemented?

  const getInitialFocusNode = function () {
    let node = getNodeForOption('initialFocus', { hasFallback: true });

    // false explicitly indicates we want no initialFocus at all
    if (node === false) {
      return false;
    }

    if (
      node === undefined ||
      (node && !isFocusable(node, config.tabbableOptions))
    ) {
      // option not specified nor focusable: use fallback options
      if (findContainerIndex(doc.activeElement) >= 0) {
        node = doc.activeElement;
      } else {
        const firstTabbableGroup = state.tabbableGroups[0];
        const firstTabbableNode =
          firstTabbableGroup && firstTabbableGroup.firstTabbableNode;

        // NOTE: `fallbackFocus` option function cannot return `false` (not supported)
        node = firstTabbableNode || getNodeForOption('fallbackFocus');
      }
    } else if (node === null) {
      // option is a VALID selector string that doesn't yield a node: use the `fallbackFocus`
      //  option instead of the default behavior when the option isn't specified at all
      node = getNodeForOption('fallbackFocus');
    }

    if (!node) {
      throw new Error(
        'Your focus-trap needs to have at least one focusable element'
      );
    }

    return node;
  };

In our case, we did not specify an initialFocus option, so it would be undefined. Then we check if the activeElement is in a container (which it isn't when we open the basket because we just clicked the open button and then the trap activates). So we go to the else branch: we look for the first tabbable group's first tabbable node.

When the basket is opened, the tabbable groups are computed for the basket container. The basket container has:
  - close button (tabbable)
  - the remove buttons for each item (tabbable)

So the first tabbable node is the close button.

Therefore, when the focused node (remove-1) is removed, the mutation observer should call tryFocus(getInitialFocusNode()) which would try to focus the close button.

But why didn't it work?

Looking at the observed state after the removal: the focus is on the body. The mutation observer might have run, but note that the removal of the li also removes the button. The mutation observer is set on the container (the basket) and its subtree. The removal of the li is a childList change, so it should be caught.

However, note that the mutation observer is disconnected and reconnected in updateObservedNodes. We see in the activate function:

      await addListeners();
      trap._setSubtreeIsolation(true);
      updateObservedNodes();

And in deactivate and pause/unpause we update the observed nodes.

But in the click handler for the remove button, we remove the li and then update the count. This removal happens synchronously. The mutation observer should catch it.

But note: the mutation observer callback is checkDomRemoval, which calls tryFocus(getInitialFocusNode()). However, the tryFocus function might not be working as expected because the focus trap might be paused? Or the state might be inconsistent.

Alternatively, note that the observed state shows that the basket is still modal and the backdrop is not hidden. The focus is on the body, which is behind the backdrop. The backdrop has a class that sets display: flex when not hidden, and none when hidden. The backdrop is not hidden (we see it in the HTML).

But the body is not inert? Actually, when the trap is active, the backdrop sets the adjacent elements (which includes the body and everything outside the basket) to inert via aria-hidden or inert. However, in the observed state, we see:

  "inert":false

for the body. This suggests that the trap's subtree isolation (which makes the backdrop inert) is not active.

Looking at the focus-trap code, the subtree isolation is set by _setSubtreeIsolation. This is called when the trap is activated (set to true) and when deactivated or paused (set to false).

In the activate function, we have:

      await addListeners();
      trap._setSubtreeIsolation(true);
      updateObservedNodes();

So after activation, the subtree isolation should be true.

But note: the observed state shows the backdrop is not hidden and the body is not inert. Why?

Because the backdrop is made inert by setting either aria-hidden or inert on the adjacent elements. The adjacent elements are collected by collectAdjacentElements, which includes the body and other elements outside the basket.

In the CSS, we have:

  .backdrop{position:fixed;inset:0;background:rgba(0,0,0,.55);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center}
  .backdrop[hidden]{display:none}

But note: the backdrop element itself is not made inert. The adjacent elements (which are the siblings of the basket container and its ancestors) are made inert.

The basket container is inside the backdrop. The backdrop is fixed and covers the whole viewport. The adjacent elements would be nothing? Because the backdrop is fixed and inset:0, so it covers the viewport and there are no siblings.

Wait: the backdrop is a div with id="backdrop". It is a sibling of the main element. The basket is inside the backdrop.

So the adjacent elements for the basket container (which is inside the backdrop) would be:
  - The backdrop's siblings: the main element and possibly others? But note the backdrop is fixed and inset:0, so it is positioned relative to the viewport.

The collectAdjacentElements function collects:
  - All ancestors of the containers (the basket container) and their siblings.

The basket container's ancestors: 
  - The backdrop (div#backdrop)
  - The body

Then, for each ancestor, we add the siblings of that ancestor.

For the backdrop (which is a child of body):
  - Its siblings: the main element.

For the body:
  - Its siblings: none (because body is the root).

So the adjacent elements would be the main element.

Then, when the trap is active, we set the adjacent elements (the main element) to inert (via aria-hidden or inert). The body itself is not made inert.

But note: the body is an ancestor of the basket container, and we do not mark the ancestors themselves as inert, only their siblings.

Therefore, the body is not inert. This explains why the focus can be on the body.

Now, why did the focus escape to the body? Because when the button was removed, the browser moved the focus to the body (as the next thing in the tab order that is not inert?).

But the focus trap should have prevented that by moving the focus back to the close button via the mutation observer.

It seems the mutation observer did not work. Let's check the code for the mutation observer:

  const mutationObserver =
    typeof window !== 'undefined' && 'MutationObserver' in window
      ? new MutationObserver(checkDomRemoval)
      : undefined;

  const updateObservedNodes = function () {
    if (!mutationObserver) {
      return;
    }

    mutationObserver.disconnect();
    if (state.active && !state.paused) {
      state.containers.map(function (container) {
        mutationObserver.observe(container, {
          subtree: true,
          childList: true,
        });
      });
    }
  };

We see that the mutation observer is set on the container (the basket) with subtree:true and childList:true. This should catch the removal of the li.

But note: the observed state shows that the basket is still active and not paused. So the mutation observer should be connected.

However, note that the removal of the li happens in a click handler. The mutation observer is asynchronous? But the callback is scheduled as a microtask. The focus change due to removal of the focused element happens synchronously? 

Actually, when an element that has focus is removed, the browser will move the focus to the body (or the next focusable element) synchronously. Then the mutation observer callback runs later.

In the observed state, we are looking at the state after the click event has been processed. The mutation observer callback might not have run yet? But the test framework might be waiting for microtasks? We don't know.

Alternatively, note that the observed state shows:

  "focusEvents":[{"from":"body","sequence":0,"tag":"button","to":"#open"},{"from":"#open","sequence":1,"tag":"button","to":"#close"},{"from":"#close","sequence":2,"tag":"button","to":"#remove-1"}]

This shows that the focus went from #close to #remove-1 on the Tab press (step 2). Then on step 3, we pressed Enter on #remove-1, but there is no focus event recorded for the removal? 

The focusEvents array only shows three events. The removal of the button and the subsequent focus move to the body is not captured as a focus event? 

But note: the focusIn event is used by the trap to track focus. The checkFocusIn function is set as a listener for focusin on the document. When the focus moves to the body, that should trigger a focusin event on the body, and then the trap's checkFocusIn function would run.

In checkFocusIn:

  const checkFocusIn = function (event) {
    const target = getActualTarget(event);
    const targetContained = findContainerIndex(target, event) >= 0;

    // In Firefox when you Tab out of an iframe the Document is briefly focused.
    if (targetContained || target instanceof Document) {
      if (targetContained) {
        state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode = target;
      }
    } else {
      // escaped! pull it back in to where it just left
      event.stopImmediatePropagation();

      // focus will escape if the MRU node had a positive tab index and user tried to nav forward;
      //  it will also escape if the MRU node had a 0 tab index and user tried to nav backward
      //  toward a node with a positive tab index
      let nextNode; // next node to focus, if we find one
      let navAcrossContainers = true;
      if (state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode) {
        if (getTabIndex(state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode) > 0) {
          // MRU container index must be >=0 otherwise we wouldn't have it as an MRU node...
          const mruContainerIdx = findContainerIndex(
            state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode
          );
          // there MAY not be any tabbable nodes in the container if there are at least 2 containers
          //  and the MRU node is focusable but not tabbable (focus-trap requires at least 1 container
          //  with at least one tabbable node in order to function, so this could be the other container
          //  with nothing tabbable in it)
          const { tabbableNodes } = state.containerGroups[mruContainerIdx];
          if (tabbableNodes.length > 0) {
            // MRU tab index MAY not be found if the MRU node is focusable but not tabbable
            const mruTabIdx = tabbableNodes.findIndex(
              (node) => node === state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode
            );
            if (mruTabIdx >= 0) {
              if (config.isKeyForward(state.recentNavEvent)) {
                if (mruTabIdx + 1 < tabbableNodes.length) {
                  nextNode = tabbableNodes[mruTabIdx + 1];
                  navAcrossContainers = false;
                }
                // else, don't wrap within the container as focus should move to next/previous
                //  container
              } else {
                if (mruTabIdx - 1 >= 0) {
                  nextNode = tabbableNodes[mruTabIdx - 1];
                  navAcrossContainers = false;
                }
                // else, don't wrap within the container as focus should move to next/previous
                //  container
              }
              // else, don't find in container order without considering direction too
            }
          }
          // else, no tabbable nodes in that container (which means we must have at least one other
          //  container with at least one tabbable node in it, otherwise focus-trap would've thrown
          //  an error the last time updateTabbableNodes() was run): find next node among all known
          //  containers
        } else {
          // check to see if there's at least one tabbable node with a positive tab index inside
          //  the trap because focus seems to escape when navigating backward from a tabbable node
          //  with tabindex=0 when this is the case (instead of wrapping to the tabbable node with
          //  the greatest positive tab index like it should)
          if (
            !state.containerGroups.some((g) =>
              g.tabbableNodes.some((n) => getTabIndex(n) > 0)
            )
          ) {
            // no containers with tabbable nodes with positive tab indexes which means the focus
            //  escaped for some other reason and we should just execute the fallback to the
            //  MRU node or initial focus node, if any
            navAcrossContainers = false;
          }
        }
      } else {
        // no MRU node means we're likely in some initial condition when the trap has just
        //  been activated and initial focus hasn't been given yet, in which case we should
        //  fall through to trying to focus the initial focus node, which is what should
        //  happen below at this point in the logic
        navAcrossContainers = false;
      }

      if (navAcrossContainers) {
        nextNode = findNextNavNode({
          // move FROM the MRU node, not event-related node (which will be the node that is
          //  outside the trap causing the focus escape we're trying to fix)
          target: state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode,
          isBackward: config.isKeyBackward(state.recentNavEvent),
        });
      }

      if (nextNode) {
        tryFocus(nextNode);
      } else {
        tryFocus(state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode || getInitialFocusNode());
      }
    }

    state.recentNavEvent = undefined; // clear
  };

This function is designed to catch focus escapes and pull the focus back in. It uses the most recently focused node (state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode) to determine where to put the focus.

In our case, when the button is removed, the focus moves to the body. This triggers a focusin event on the body. The target (body) is not contained in the basket container (findContainerIndex returns -1) and is not a Document, so we go into the else branch.

Then we try to compute a nextNode to focus. We use the state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode, which at that point should be the button that was just removed (because the focusin event fires after the focus has changed, but the trap updates state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode in the tryFocus function and also in the focusin handler when the target is contained).

But note: in the tryFocus function, we set state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode = node. And in the focusin handler, when the target is contained, we set state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode = target.

However, when the focus moves to the body (which is not contained), we do not update state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode in the if branch. But we do have the MRU node from before the escape.

In the escape handling, we use state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode to compute where to put the focus back.

What is state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode at the time of the escape? It should be the button that was removed (because we had just focused it and then it was removed, but the focusin event for the body happens after the removal and after the focus has moved to the body). However, note that the trap's tryFocus function is called when we focus the button, and that sets state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode to the button. Then when the button is removed and the focus moves to the body, the focusin event for the body fires. At that point, state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode is still the button (because we haven't updated it for the body focusin event since it's not contained).

So in the escape handling, we have state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode set to the removed button.

Now, we try to compute a nextNode. We look at the container of the MRU node (the button). The button was in the basket container. We look at the tabbable nodes in that container.

After the removal, the basket container still has two items (so two remove buttons). The tabbable nodes in the basket container are:
  - close button
  - remove-2 button
  - remove-3 button

The MRU node (the removed button) was the first remove button. In the tabbableNodes array for the basket container, it was at index 1 (if we count: [close, remove-1, remove-2, remove-3] -> but note: remove-1 is gone, so after removal the array is [close, remove-2, remove-3]).

But note: the mutation observer has not run yet to update the tabbableNodes? The escape handling happens synchronously in the focusin event, and the mutation observer is asynchronous.

Therefore, at the time of the focusin event, the state.tabbableGroups and state.containerGroups have not been updated to reflect the removal. They still represent the state before the removal.

So the container group for the basket still shows the tabbableNodes as including the removed button.

We then try to find the index of the MRU node (the removed button) in the tabbableNodes array. We find it (at index 1, assuming the close button is index0 and the removed button is index1).

Then, because we are handling a focus escape that happened without a key event (it was a mouse click that removed the element, which caused the focus to move to the body), we look at the recentNavEvent. But note: the recentNavEvent is set only in the key handlers (checkTabKey and checkEscapeKey). We had a click event, not a key event. So state.recentNavEvent is undefined.

In the escape handling, we have:

      if (state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode) {
        if (getTabIndex(state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode) > 0) {
          ... // we try to find the next node in the container's tabbableNodes
        } else {
          ... // check for positive tabindex in any container
        }
      } else {
        ... // no MRU node
      }

The removed button had a tabindex of 0 (because it's a button without a tabindex attribute). So getTabIndex returns 0.

Therefore, we go into the else branch:

          // check to see if there's at least one tabbable node with a positive tab index inside
          //  the trap because focus seems to escape when navigating backward from a tabbable node
          //  with tabindex=0 when this is the case (instead of wrapping to the tabbable node with
          //  the greatest positive tab index like it should)
          if (
            !state.containerGroups.some((g) =>
              g.tabbableNodes.some((n) => getTabIndex(n) > 0)
            )
          ) {
            // no containers with tabbable nodes with positive tab indexes which means the focus
            //  escaped for some other reason and we should just execute the fallback to the
            //  MRU node or initial focus node, if any
            navAcrossContainers = false;
          }

In our basket, all the buttons (close and remove) have tabindex 0 (by default). So there are no tabbable nodes with positive tabindex. Therefore, navAcrossContainers is set to false.

Then we do:

      if (navAcrossContainers) {
        nextNode = findNextNavNode({
          target: state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode,
          isBackward: config.isKeyBackward(state.recentNavEvent),
        });
      } else {
        // we don't set nextNode here, so it remains null
      }

Then:

      if (nextNode) {
        tryFocus(nextNode);
      } else {
        tryFocus(state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode || getInitialFocusNode());
      }

Since nextNode is null, we try to focus state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode (the removed button) or getInitialFocusNode().

But the removed button is no longer in the DOM, so trying to focus it will fail and then we fall back to getInitialFocusNode().

The getInitialFocusNode() function, as we saw, returns the first tabbable node in the basket (which is the close button) because we don't have an initialFocus option and the activeElement is not in a container (the activeElement is the body at the time of the call? but note: we are in the focusin handler for the body, so the activeElement is the body, and then we try to focus the initial focus node).

So we should end up focusing the close button.

But why didn't we? The observed state shows the focus on the body.

This suggests that the tryFocus call to getInitialFocusNode() did not work. Let's look at tryFocus:

  const tryFocus = function (node) {
    if (node === false) {
      return;
    }

    if (node === getActiveElement(document)) {
      return;
    }

    if (!node || !node.focus) {
      tryFocus(getInitialFocusNode());
      return;
    }

    node.focus({ preventScroll: !!config.preventScroll });
    // NOTE: focus() API does not trigger focusIn event so set MRU node manually
    state.mostRecentlyFocusedNode = node;

    if (isSelectableInput(node)) {
      node.select();
    }
  };

We are calling tryFocus(getInitialFocusNode()). The getInitialFocusNode() returns the close button (which is a focusable node and has a focus method). So we should call focus on the close button.

But note: the close button is inside the basket, which is inside the backdrop. The backdrop is not hidden. The close button should be focusable.

However, observe the observed state: the backdrop is not hidden and the basket is visible. The close button is present.

But note: the backdrop has a style that makes it a flex container and centers the basket. The close button is inside the basket's header.

Why didn't the focus move to the close button?

One possibility: the focus trap's subtree isolation is not active, so the close button is not focusable? But we saw that the body is not inert, and the backdrop is not made inert. Only the adjacent elements (the main element) are made inert. The close button is inside the backdrop, which is not an adjacent element (it's an ancestor of the container?).

Wait: the basket container is inside the backdrop. The backdrop is an ancestor of the basket container. The adjacent elements are the siblings of the ancestors. The backdrop's sibling is the main element. So the main element is made inert. The backdrop itself is not made inert, and neither are its children (the basket and its contents).

Therefore, the close button should be focusable.

Another possibility: the tryFocus call is happening, but then immediately after, something else is moving the focus to the body? We don't see any other event.

Alternatively, note that the observed state shows:

  "focus":{"bbox":{"height":201.21875,"width":1280,"x":0,"y":0},"disabled":false,"html":"<body> ... </body>","inViewport":true,"inert":false,"modal":null,"nameHint":"Garden store\nView basket\nBasket\n×\n\n2 items\n\nPruning shears\nRemove\nKneeling pad\nRemove","obscuredAtCenter":false,"outline":{"color":"rgb(17, 17, 17)","style":"none","width":"3px"},"role":null,"selector":"body","tag":"body","visible":true}

This indicates that the focus is on the body, and the body's computed outline is none (so no focus ring). But the body is not supposed to be focusable? Actually, the body is not focusable by default, but it can be given focus programmatically.

Given the complexity, let's consider a simpler solution: when an item is removed, we should explicitly move the focus to the next item or to the close button if there are no items left? But note the count is updated to "2 items", so there are still items.

However, the requirement is: after removing a row, keyboard focus should remain inside the trapped dialog.

We can fix this by ensuring that when we remove the focused item, we move the focus to the next focusable item in the list (or the previous one if we are at the end) or to the close button if the list becomes empty.

But note: the focus trap should already handle this via the mutation observer and the focusin handler. The fact that it didn't work suggests a bug in the focus-trap implementation when the focused element is removed and it has tabindex 0.

Looking at the focus-trap code, we see that in the escape handling, when the MRU node has tabindex 0 and there are no positive tab indexes in the trap, we set navAcrossContainers to false and then try to focus the MRU node (which is gone) or the initial focus node.

But why didn't the initial focus node work? It might be because the initial focus node (the close button) is not focusable at that exact moment? Or perhaps the tryFocus call is being interrupted.

Alternatively, note that the observed state shows that the basket is still present and has two items. The close button is there.

Let's try to simulate: after removing the first item, we call tryFocus(getInitialFocusNode()) which tries to focus the close button. This should succeed and then we would see a focus event on the close button. But we don't see one in the focusEvents array.

The focusEvents array only has three events. The fourth event (the focus on the close button after the removal) is missing.

This suggests that the tryFocus call did not actually change the focus.

Why would tryFocus not change the focus? 

  if (node === getActiveElement(document)) {
    return;
  }

